# tune2fs — real-world examples

Tune parameters on ext2/ext3/ext4 filesystems. Most operations need root
and are safest on unmounted filesystems.

## Inspect a filesystem

```sh
# List all tunable parameters (UUID, label, mount count, features...)
sudo tune2fs -l /dev/sda2

# Homelab: check when the media drive was last fsck'd
sudo tune2fs -l /dev/sdb1 | grep -i 'last checked'
```

## Add or remove a journal

```sh
# Add a journal (upgrades ext2 -> ext3 behavior)
sudo tune2fs -j /dev/sdb1

# Remove the journal (filesystem must be unmounted)
sudo tune2fs -O ^has_journal /dev/sdb1
```

## Labels and UUIDs

```sh
# Set a label — handy for /etc/fstab entries like LABEL=media
sudo tune2fs -L media /dev/sdb1

# Assign a new random UUID (e.g. after cloning a disk with dd)
sudo tune2fs -U random /dev/sdb1
```

## Reserved blocks and check intervals

```sh
# Drop reserved space from 5% to 1% on a big data-only drive
# (frees ~40 GB on a 1 TB Plex media disk; keep 5% on the root fs)
sudo tune2fs -m 1 /dev/sdb1

# Force fsck every 30 mounts or every 3 months, whichever comes first
sudo tune2fs -c 30 -i 3m /dev/sda2

# Disable mount-count and time-based checks entirely
sudo tune2fs -c 0 -i 0 /dev/sdb1
```

## Flag reference

| Flag | Meaning |
|------|---------|
| `-l` | list filesystem parameters |
| `-j` | add an ext3 journal |
| `-O [^]feature` | set/clear a feature (`^` clears, e.g. `^has_journal`) |
| `-L LABEL` | set volume label |
| `-U UUID` | set UUID (`random`, `time`, or explicit) |
| `-m PCT` | percentage of blocks reserved for root |
| `-c N` | max mount count between checks (0 = disable) |
| `-i INTERVAL` | max time between checks (e.g. `10d`, `3m`) |
| `-e BEHAVIOR` | on-error behavior: `continue`, `remount-ro`, `panic` |
